." mkdir(1)
.TH MKDIR 1 "April 2026" "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mkdir \- create directories
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mkdir
.RI [ OPTION ]... DIRECTORY ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mkdir
creates each named
.IR DIRECTORY .
By default, each operand names a single directory that must not already exist.
With
.BR -p ,
missing parent directories are created as needed and an existing directory does not cause failure.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -m , " --mode=" MODE
Set the final mode of each created directory.
.I MODE
may be an octal value such as
.B 755
or a symbolic mode such as
.BR u=rwx,go= .
The mode is applied after creation so the final directory mode does not depend on the process umask.
.TP
.BR -p , " --parents"
Create parent directories as needed.
Existing directories are accepted.
Intermediate directories are created with the default directory mode and the process umask still applies to those intermediate components.
.TP
.BR -v , " --verbose"
Print one line for each directory that is actually created.
.TP
.BR -Z , " --context" [ =CTX ]
Request SELinux context handling.
Current Substrate builds accept the option as a stub and print a warning that SELinux contexts are ignored.
.TP
.B --help
Display usage information and exit.
.TP
.B --version
Display version information and exit.
.SH MODE SEMANTICS
Without
.BR -m ,
directories are created with mode
.B 0777
subject to the process umask.
.PP
With
.BR -m ,
the supplied mode is interpreted relative to a directory base mode of
.BR 0777 .
Numeric modes replace the permission bits directly.
Symbolic modes support:
.TP
- WHO classes:
.BR u , g , o , a
.TP
- operations:
.BR + , - , =
.TP
- permissions:
.BR r , w , x , X , s , t
.PP
Multiple symbolic clauses separated by commas are applied from left to right.
.SH UMASK AND PARENTS
When
.B mkdir
creates a directory without
.BR -m ,
the kernel applies the process umask during the
.BR mkdir (2)
operation.
.PP
When
.BR -m
is present, the final target directory is created first and then adjusted with
.BR fchmod (2)
so the resulting mode matches the requested value even if the umask would otherwise mask bits.
.PP
With
.BR -p ,
intermediate directories are created with default permissions and are not post-adjusted by
.BR -m .
If the final operand already exists as a directory, the command succeeds and leaves its current mode unchanged.
.SH PATH HANDLING
Path processing is component-based and does not rely on fixed-size path buffers.
Repeated slashes are ignored.
Absolute paths, relative paths, trailing slashes, and
.B .
or
.B ..
components are handled according to normal pathname resolution rules.
.PP
If any component in a
.B -p
path exists but is not a directory, the operation fails for that operand.
.SH RACE SAFETY
For
.BR -p ,
Substrate
.B mkdir
creates components with
.BR mkdirat (2)
and validates pre-existing components with
.BR fstatat (2)
before descending with
.BR openat (2).
This keeps pathname traversal relative to directory file descriptors and allows concurrent
.B mkdir -p
operations on the same path to converge successfully when all conflicting components are directories.
.SH OWNERSHIP
Created directories are owned by the effective user and group IDs selected by the kernel and filesystem.
If a parent directory has the setgid bit set, child group inheritance follows the underlying filesystem semantics.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
All requested directories were created successfully, or already existed when
.B -p
was used.
.TP
1
At least one operand failed.
The command continues processing later operands after per-path errors.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
mkdir project
Create a single directory.
.TP
mkdir -p src/lib/tests
Create a nested directory hierarchy.
.TP
mkdir -m 700 private
Create a directory accessible only to its owner.
.TP
mkdir -m u=rwx,go= secure
Create a directory using a symbolic mode expression.
.TP
mkdir -pv build/output
Create a nested directory and print each component that was created.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR chmod (1),
.BR rmdir (1),
.BR stat (2),
.BR mkdir (2),
.BR mkdirat (2),
.BR fstatat (2),
.BR openat (2),
.BR umask (2)